## CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION AND PROBLEM STATEMENT

### 1.1 A New Sub-Discipline

This dissertation contributes to the founding of a new academic sub-discipline: **Extraterrestrial Macroeconomics**. This field sits at the intersection of labor economics, growth theory, space systems engineering, astrophysical constraints, and institutional design. Its central object of study is the economy of a civilization that spans more than one world and asks how such a civilization should allocate productive capacity, human labor, and capital across planetary boundaries.

We arrive at this sub-discipline not from aspiration but from necessity. The accelerating capability of artificial intelligence systems creates a condition that no existing macroeconomic theory adequately addresses: the approach of a state in which AI performs virtually all economically valuable tasks at lower cost than human labor. Classical growth theory treats technology as an exogenous blessing that raises all wages. Endogenous growth theory endogenizes technological progress but assumes humans remain productive within the new knowledge-intensive tasks AI creates. The task-based displacement framework of Acemoglu and Restrepo is the most sophisticated available tool, yet it too assumes that historical reinstatement rates can be sustained into the deep-automation regime.

We argue that none of these frameworks survive the transition to full task automation (θ(t) → 1) with their employment-sustaining properties intact. Reinstatement of human labor within a closed terrestrial economy is mathematically impossible beyond a threshold of AI capability we call T*. The only framework that can sustain human employment at civilizational scale beyond T* is the expansion of civilizational scope: the opening of a frontier whose task-space complexity structurally exceeds θ(t) by a margin that increases with frontier breadth.

### 1.2 Identified Vulnerabilities and Responses

The pre-defense committee appraisal identified three major theoretical vulnerabilities in the first edition of this dissertation:

**Vulnerability 1 — The Automation Paradox of the Frontier.** If AI capability scales to produce 30–100% annual GDP growth on Earth, why would it fail to automate lunar mining and Martian construction? The Frontier Employment Theorem requires a formal proof that the frontier is a structural human labor sanctuary — not merely a temporary one.

**Vulnerability 2 — Scalar Infrastructure Aggregation.** Treating infrastructure stock I(t) as a scalar measured in kilograms of off-Earth material ignores the thermodynamic and economic heterogeneity of space assets. A ton of regolith shielding is economically non-equivalent to a kilogram of precision life-support machinery.

**Vulnerability 3 — Sovereign Tax Arbitrage.** The CDF revenue model assumes a global levy τ on AI capital rents. Without a formal proof that sovereign states cannot profitably defect, the CDF is underfunded before Phase 2 initiates.

Each vulnerability is addressed in a dedicated new section: Vulnerability 1 in Chapter 4 (via the Knightian Uncertainty Formalization), Vulnerability 2 in the revised Chapter 5 (via vector-valued infrastructure decomposition), and Vulnerability 3 in Chapter 7 (via the Game-Theoretic Sovereign Co-opetition Module).

### 1.3 Structure

Chapter 2 surveys the mathematical literature. Chapter 3 develops the AI Labor Displacement Module, including the accelerating Labor Force Participation curve. Chapter 4 formalizes the Frontier Sanctuary via latency bounds and OOD degradation. Chapter 5 presents the Civilization Capability Dynamic System with full Jacobian stability analysis and the vector infrastructure correction. Chapter 6 constructs the Dual-Economy Architecture and derives the Optimal Space-Compute Threshold (Phase 1 → Phase 2 transition). Chapter 7 develops the Civilization Development Fund, Civilization Bonds, and the Game-Theoretic Sovereign Co-opetition Module. Chapter 8 provides the four-phase Strategic Roadmap with mathematically specified transition conditions for each boundary. Chapter 9 discusses limitations and counterarguments. Chapter 10 concludes.

