## CHAPTER 2: MATHEMATICAL LITERATURE REVIEW

### 2.1 Classical Growth: The Solow-Swan Baseline

The Solow-Swan production function:

$$Y = A \cdot K^{\alpha} \cdot L^{1-\alpha}$$

captures output Y as a function of exogenous technology A, capital K, and labor L. The Solow-Swan model demonstrated that capital accumulation alone cannot sustain long-run growth due to diminishing returns; the economy converges to a steady state where output grows only with exogenous technological progress. This explains convergence but leaves the ultimate source of growth unexplained. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.16261)

For our purposes, the Solow framework fails in two distinct ways. First, it has no mechanism for the displacement of labor by a technology that substitutes for L rather than augmenting it. Second, it offers no theory of where exogenous A comes from or how civilizational scope expands A's effective domain. Both failures are remedied by the frameworks below.

### 2.2 Endogenous Growth: Knowledge as the Engine

Endogenous growth theory fills the Solow gap by recognizing that knowledge differs from physical capital: ideas are non-rival and potentially non-excludable, creating increasing returns at the aggregate level. Romer's model formalized how firms invest in R&D to develop ideas that create spillovers benefiting others. Key mechanisms include learning by doing, human capital, R&D, knowledge spillovers, and increasing returns. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.04543)

In Romer's (1990) formulation, knowledge accumulation is:

$$\dot{A} = \delta \cdot H_A \cdot A$$

where H_A is human capital in research and δ is research productivity. The implication we exploit is that if AI displaces human capital from production, that capital *must be redirected* — not to UBI recipients but to frontier research and construction tasks where AI cannot yet operate competitively.

### 2.3 The Task-Based Framework

The task-based framework, pioneered by Zeira (1998) and developed by Acemoglu and Autor (2011) and Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018–2024), opens the production black box by decomposing output into tasks performed by either labor or capital, allowing direct analysis of substitution and complementarity. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.15265)

Production is a CES aggregate over a continuum of tasks z ∈ [0, 1]:

$$Y = \left( \int_0^1 y(z)^{\frac{\sigma-1}{\sigma}} dz \right)^{\frac{\sigma}{\sigma-1}}$$

Technological progress takes two primary forms: automation, where capital takes over tasks previously performed by labor, creating a labor-displacing effect; and the creation of new, more complex tasks in which labor has a comparative advantage, creating a labor-reinstating effect. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.00068)

The critical insight of the revised dissertation is that the magnitude of any terrestrial reinstatement event is bounded — while the magnitude of a frontier-based reinstatement event is effectively unbounded. This asymmetry is what makes space colonization structurally different from, say, the Internet as a reinstatement mechanism.

### 2.4 The GATE Model

The GATE model combines three key ingredients: a compute-based model of AI development, an AI automation framework, and a semi-endogenous growth model featuring endogenous investment and adjustment costs, allowing users to simulate the economic effects of the transition to advanced AI across a range of potential scenarios. [ADS](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025arXiv250304941E/abstract)

GATE suggests the world will make massive investments in AI compute, potentially exceeding 10% of GDP, and that even under relatively conservative assumptions about returns to hardware and software R&D, compute scaling could lead to the automation of labor tasks within 20 years, with high levels of automation leading to GDP growth potentially reaching 30–100% per year. [Substack](https://epochai.substack.com/p/the-epoch-ai-brief-april-2025)

This projection defines the surplus that must be redirected. Our dissertation derives the minimum fraction of this surplus that must flow toward the Civilization Economy to sustain human employment during and after the displacement completion event.

### 2.5 The Kardashev Civilizational Index

Humanity presently stands at approximately Type 0.7276 on the Kardashev Scale, which quantifies the relationship between energy consumption and civilizational development. [Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-38351-y) If energy strategies and technologies remain on the present course, it may take human civilization millennia to become a Type 1 civilization. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10338519/)

We show that under Steady State 2 of our dynamic system — AI surplus redirected into space infrastructure — the Kardashev trajectory accelerates by multiple orders of magnitude, approaching Type 1 by 2100–2150. This connects the unemployment problem to the deepest level of civilizational physics: energy availability determines both the ceiling of human activity and the maximum complexity of the problems humans can pursue.

### 2.6 The Space Economy Baseline

The global space economy reached revenues of $570 billion in 2023, reflecting a 7.4% year-over-year increase in line with a five-year CAGR of 7.3%, and nearly double the total from a decade ago. Future projections suggest the global space economy may grow to as much as $2 trillion by 2040. [PwC](https://www.pwc.com/us/en/industries/industrial-products/library/space-industry-trends.html) More optimistic long-range projections estimate the space economy reaching $2.7 trillion by 2045. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0265964625000347)

These figures represent the *no-surplus-redirection baseline*. The analysis in Chapters 6 and 7 demonstrates that deliberate AI-surplus redirection can grow the Civilization Economy to an order of magnitude beyond these baseline projections within the Phase 2–3 timeframe.

