## CHAPTER 3: THE AI LABOR DISPLACEMENT MODULE

### 3.1 The Task-Space Automaton

We model the economy as a continuum of tasks indexed by z ∈ [0, 1], ordered from simplest (z → 0) to most cognitively and physically complex (z → 1). The displacement threshold function θ(t) marks the boundary below which AI has achieved cost competitiveness with human labor:

$$\theta(t) = \sup\{z : c_{AI}(z,t) \leq w(z,t)\}$$

Human employment demand is:

$$H(t) = \int_{\theta(t)}^{1} L(z, t) \, dz$$

The displacement rate is:

$$\dot{H}(t) = -L(\theta(t), t) \cdot \dot{\theta}(t) + \int_{\theta(t)}^{1} \dot{L}(z, t) \, dz$$

The first term (negative) is the displacement effect. The second term is the reinstatement effect. We define the **Reinstatement Gap** as:

$$G(t) = \frac{d\theta}{dt} - \frac{d\Theta_{new}}{dt}$$

When G(t) > 0, displacement outpaces new task creation and structural unemployment grows. The central thesis is that G(t) < 0 can only be guaranteed, in the full-automation regime, by expanding the civilizational frontier F(t).

### 3.2 AI Capability Accumulation

Consistent with the GATE compute-scaling architecture:

$$\dot{A}_{AI}(t) = \lambda \cdot \left(\frac{C_{total}(t)}{C_0}\right)^{\beta} \cdot A_{AI}(t)^{\gamma}$$

As A_AI grows, θ(t) increases monotonically via:

$$\theta(t) = \Phi(A_{AI}(t)), \quad \Phi' > 0, \quad \Phi \in [0, 1]$$

Under GATE's default parameterization, θ(t) → 1 is reached within approximately 20 years, defining the *full displacement regime*. Beyond this horizon, all domestic reinstatement mechanisms are exhausted within the closed terrestrial economy.

### 3.3 The Accelerating Labor Force Participation Curve

A critical structural refinement from the committee appraisal: the drop in labor force participation (LFP) is not linear. We model it as an accelerating curve that bends sharply once agentic multimodal systems cross the threshold of human error rates in complex physical-cognitive labor:

$$N_{LFP}(t) = N_0 \cdot \exp\left(-\int_0^t \mu(\theta(s)) \, ds\right)$$

where the exit hazard rate μ(θ) is:

$$\mu(\theta) = \mu_0 + \frac{\mu_1}{1 + e^{-k(\theta - \theta_c)}}$$

This is a logistic acceleration function. At low θ (partial automation), exit hazard is baseline μ₀. As θ crosses the critical cognitive threshold θ_c, the hazard accelerates sharply due to the rapid obsolescence of cognitive task skills. The labor force participation rate is projected to fall from approximately 62.6% in 2025 to around 61% by 2030 and as low as 55% by 2050, with people exiting the labor force entirely rather than remaining unemployed. [AIMultiple](https://aimultiple.com/ai-job-loss) Our logistic model fits this trajectory and extrapolates its post-2050 acceleration.

The policy target is to make N_CivE(t) — employment in the Civilization Economy — grow faster than N_LFP declines in the terrestrial economy, a condition we formalize in Chapter 4.

### 3.4 The Reinstatement Deficit in the Full-Automation Regime

Economic models suggest that once AI can perform 30–40% of all economically valuable tasks, annual growth rates could exceed 20%, but this growth might primarily benefit capital owners rather than workers, exacerbating power concentration and existing inequalities. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13700)

As θ(t) → 1, the terrestrial reinstatement mechanism collapses for a mathematical reason: every new task created in the Consumer Economy is itself automatable at speed, because AI learns from observing human performance of the new task almost immediately. The reinstatement gap G(t) grows without bound in the closed terrestrial system. Only a domain with structural AI incompetence — formalized in Chapter 4 — can arrest this divergence.

