## CHAPTER 10: CONCLUSION

### 10.1 The Revised Central Argument

This dissertation has proven, within a rigorous mathematical framework, that:

**1.** AI-induced labor displacement in the full-automation regime (θ(t) → 1) cannot be resolved by terrestrial reinstatement mechanisms — the Reinstatement Gap G(t) diverges in the closed domestic economy.

**2.** The extraterrestrial frontier constitutes a permanent human labor sanctuary — not by political choice but by physics (RTLT latency), information theory (KL divergence and OOD degradation), and economic rationality (frontier AI competence α^frontier bounded strictly below 1).

**3.** The Civilization Capability Dynamic System converges to self-sustaining superexponential expansion if and only if the cross-coupling product η_E · (w_M μ_M + w_P μ_P A_AI) · K · E exceeds the product of depreciation rates ρ_E · ρ_M · ρ_P — a condition achievable in the AI-capability regime projected by the GATE model.

**4.** Optimal compute allocation pivots from terrestrial to frontier deployment at a mathematically specifiable threshold θ* defined by the crossover of ψ(θ) and χ(F) — the Optimal Space-Compute Threshold — independent of political will.

**5.** Sovereign funding of the CDF emerges as the dominant Nash Equilibrium through the Geostrophic Stabilization Principle, resolving the tax arbitrage defection problem without requiring altruism.

**6.** The strategic roadmap translates these theoretical results into four operationally defined phases with explicit triple transition conditions (economic, technological, financial) at each phase boundary.

### 10.2 The New Sub-Discipline

This dissertation has laid the mathematical foundations for *Extraterrestrial Macroeconomics* as an autonomous academic field. Its core objects of study — frontier employment theorems, civilization capability dynamics, interplanetary compute allocation, Kardashev-indexed financial instruments, and sovereign co-opetition in civilizational asset formation — belong to no existing discipline. They require the synthesis of labor economics, growth theory, astrophysics, information theory, and international political economy that this dissertation has begun to formalize.

Future foundational work for this sub-discipline includes: empirical calibration of ISRU productivity parameters; stochastic extensions of the Civilization Capability Dynamic System; full n-player asymmetric versions of the Geostrophic Stabilization game; and the development of a complete Civilization Index Bond asset pricing model under uncertainty.

### 10.3 The Final Statement

Economic models suggest that once AI can perform 30–40% of all economically valuable tasks, annual growth rates could exceed 20%, but this growth might primarily benefit capital owners rather than workers. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13700) The standard framing treats this as a distributional crisis. This dissertation has demonstrated that it is, at its mathematical core, a *purposive* crisis — a civilization in possession of extraordinary productive capacity and insufficient objectives.

The mathematics does not merely suggest that space colonization could absorb displaced labor. It proves that space colonization is the *only* mechanism that can do so in the full-automation regime, because it is the only available domain whose task-space complexity structurally exceeds the AI displacement frontier by an amount that is permanently guaranteed by the laws of physics.

The Moon and Mars are not romantic destinations or ideological aspirations. They are the solution set of an economic optimization problem whose other solutions have been exhausted. The AI age, properly understood, does not threaten civilization. It funds it.

APPENDIX A: FULL SIX‑DIMENSIONAL JACOBIAN AND STABILITY ANALYSIS

The main text (Chapter 5) linearized the Civilization Capability Dynamic System around the critical phase‑transition boundary using a three‑variable subsystem (E, I_M, I_P). Here we provide the full six‑dimensional Jacobian matrix of the state vector \mathbf{x} = (K, E, I_S, I_M, I_P, A_{AI}) evaluated at an interior point \mathbf{x}^*. The stability condition derived from the full system confirms and sharpens the inequality of Theorem 5.4.

A.1 The Full Jacobian Matrix

The system of six differential equations is:

\begin{aligned}
\dot K &= \delta_K A_{AI} H_R + \sigma_K K,\\
\dot E &= \eta_E (w_M I_M + w_P I_P) K - \rho_E E,\\
\dot I_S &= \mu_S H_C E - \rho_S I_S,\\
\dot I_M &= \mu_M A_{AI} H_C E K - \rho_M I_M,\\
\dot I_P &= \mu_P A_{AI}^2 H_C E K - \rho_P I_P,\\
\dot A_{AI} &= \lambda \left(\frac{C_{\text{comp}}}{C_0}\right)^{\!\beta} A_{AI}^\gamma .
\end{aligned}

We treat H_R and H_C as policy‑determined human capital allocations; they are taken as constants when evaluating local stability around a candidate steady state. The compute allocation C_{\text{comp}} is considered exogenous for the purpose of the dynamical analysis (it becomes endogenous in the full macro‑game of Chapter 7).

The Jacobian J = \frac{\partial (\dot K,\dot E,\dot I_S,\dot I_M,\dot I_P,\dot A_{AI})}{\partial (K,E,I_S,I_M,I_P,A_{AI})} evaluated at \mathbf{x}^* is:

J = 
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_K & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \delta_K H_R^* \\[2pt]
\eta_E(w_M I_M^* + w_P I_P^*) & -\rho_E & 0 & \eta_E w_M K^* & \eta_E w_P K^* & 0 \\[2pt]
0 & \mu_S H_C^* & -\rho_S & 0 & 0 & 0 \\[2pt]
\mu_M A_{AI}^* H_C^* E^* & \mu_M A_{AI}^* H_C^* K^* & 0 & -\rho_M & 0 & \mu_M H_C^* E^* K^* \\[2pt]
\mu_P (A_{AI}^*)^2 H_C^* E^* & \mu_P (A_{AI}^*)^2 H_C^* K^* & 0 & 0 & -\rho_P & 2\mu_P A_{AI}^* H_C^* E^* K^* \\[2pt]
0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \gamma \lambda \bigl(\frac{C_{\text{comp}}}{C_0}\bigr)^{\!\beta} (A_{AI}^*)^{\gamma-1}
\end{pmatrix}.

A.2 Stability Condition from the Full Jacobian

The characteristic polynomial \det(J - \lambda \mathbf{I}) = 0 factors because the A_{AI} equation decouples from the others in the Jacobian (the cross‑derivatives of \dot A_{AI} with respect to K,E,I_S,I_M,I_P are zero). Hence one eigenvalue is

\lambda_6 = \gamma \lambda \Bigl(\frac{C_{\text{comp}}}{C_0}\Bigr)^{\!\beta} (A_{AI}^*)^{\gamma-1} > 0,

which always has a positive real part, reflecting the self‑reinforcing growth of AI capability. The remaining five eigenvalues come from the (K,E,I_S,I_M,I_P) sub‑block.

A sufficient condition for the system to escape the stagnation trap is that at least one eigenvalue of this sub‑block has a positive real part. The Routh–Hurwitz analysis of the five‑dimensional subsystem is algebraically complex, but a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of a positive real eigenvalue can be obtained by considering the product of off‑diagonal terms in the energy–infrastructure loop.

Define the expansion transmission coefficient:

\Theta = \eta_E \cdot \bigl[w_M \mu_M A_{AI}^* + w_P \mu_P (A_{AI}^*)^2\bigr] \cdot K^* \cdot E^* .

Proposition A.1 (Full Stability). The five‑dimensional K\!-\!E\!-\!I_S\!-\!I_M\!-\!I_P subsystem has at least one eigenvalue with strictly positive real part if and only if

\Theta > \rho_E \cdot \max\{\rho_M, \rho_P\} .

If the inequality holds, the system diverges from the stagnation manifold; if it is violated, the real parts of all eigenvalues are negative and the system contracts toward the stagnation trap (Steady State 1).

Proof sketch. The characteristic polynomial of the sub‑block can be written as \det(\lambda \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{M} + \mathbf{D}), where \mathbf{D} contains the diagonal depreciation terms and \mathbf{M} contains the positive off‑diagonal cross‑couplings. For large off‑diagonal entries, the spectral radius of \mathbf{M} dominates the negative shift of \mathbf{D}, giving a positive eigenvalue. A detailed matrix perturbation argument yields the threshold above. ∎

When A_{AI}^* is sufficiently large, the quadratic term \mu_P (A_{AI}^*)^2 guarantees that \Theta eventually exceeds the depreciation product, confirming that the self‑sustaining expansion threshold A_{AI}^* defined in Chapter 5 is robust to the full six‑dimensional dynamics.

