APPENDIX B: ASYMMETRIC MULTI‑PLAYER GEOSTROPHIC STABILIZATION

Chapter 7 derived the symmetric two‑player game and argued that the [Fund CDF] equilibrium holds in the n-player generalization. We now formalize the asymmetric case and prove that the equilibrium is unique and dominant even when players differ in size, AI capability, and initial space infrastructure.

B.1 Asymmetric Payoff Structure

Let there be N sovereign actors indexed by i = 1,\dots,N. Each actor chooses a strategy s_i \in \{\text{Fund}, \text{Defect}\}. The set of funders is \mathcal{F} = \{i : s_i = \text{Fund}\}; defectors are \mathcal{D}. The aggregate space infrastructure is \mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}} built by the funders. The value to actor i of the infrastructure depends on whether they are inside the club or excluded:

· If i \in \mathcal{F}, i enjoys full access to \mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}} and receives a continuation value V_i^{\text{in}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}}).
· If i \in \mathcal{D}, i is excluded and receives a lower value V_i^{\text{out}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}}). In particular, exclusion means no access to cislunar energy, resource stockpiles, or interplanetary logistics, and a strategic inferiority that grows with \mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}}.

The cost of funding for actor i is \tau_i R_{AI,i}, where R_{AI,i} is i’s AI capital rent. Additionally, if i defects, it can attract some AI capital from funders, yielding a short‑term gain Y_{CE,i}(\mathbf{C}_{\text{global}}) which depends on how much compute capital migrates. For simplicity we treat Y_{CE,i} as independent of the set \mathcal{F}, representing the maximum tax‑arbitrage gain under full global AI capital mobility. However, the magnitude of this gain is bounded by the global AI capital stock.

B.2 Dominance of the Fund Strategy

We impose the following empirically motivated condition:

Assumption (Space Dominance). For every actor i, the long‑run difference between the value of being inside the space infrastructure network and the value of being permanently excluded diverges to infinity as the network grows:

\lim_{t \to \infty} \bigl[ V_i^{\text{in}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(t)) - \max\{V_i^{\text{out}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}}(t)),\, Y_{CE,i}\} \bigr] = \infty .

This holds because V_i^{\text{in}} is proportional to the weighted sum of extraterrestrial infrastructure, which compounds superexponentially under the dynamics of Chapter 5, while V_i^{\text{out}} is bounded by terrestrial GDP, which itself stagnates under full automation.

Theorem B.1 (Asymmetric Dominant Strategy). Under the Space Dominance Assumption, the strategy s_i = \text{Fund} is strictly dominant for all i if the initial infrastructure stock exceeds a critical threshold \mathbf{I}_{\text{min}}.

Proof. Consider the decision of actor i given any profile of strategies for the other actors. Let \mathcal{F}_{-i} be the set of other funders. If i defects while \mathcal{F}_{-i} funds, the infrastructure \mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i}} continues to grow. After a finite time T, the gap V_i^{\text{in}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i} \cup \{i\}}) - V_i^{\text{out}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i}}) will exceed the short‑term tax arbitrage gain Y_{CE,i}, because the former diverges. Discounting at rate r>0, the present value of the decision can be written as

\text{PV(Defect)} = \int_0^T e^{-rt} Y_{CE,i}\,dt + \int_T^\infty e^{-rt} V_i^{\text{out}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i}}(t))\,dt,

\text{PV(Fund)} = -\tau_i R_{AI,i}(t) \text{ for } t \in [0,T) + \int_0^\infty e^{-rt} V_i^{\text{in}}(\mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i} \cup \{i\}}(t))\,dt .

For sufficiently large \mathbf{I}_{\mathcal{F}_{-i}}(0) (guaranteed by the initial condition \mathbf{I}_{\text{min}}), the future value premium of being inside dominates the initial levy cost and any arbitrage gain. Hence PV(Fund) > PV(Defect). If all others defect, \mathcal{F}_{-i} = \emptyset, then defection yields the stagnation trap payoff for all, while funding unilaterally yields the same trap except that i bears the levy cost. However, the assumption \mathbf{I}_{\text{min}} ensures that a unilateral funder can build enough infrastructure (with the AI surplus it still controls) to eventually dominate, making Fund still optimal. Detailed backward induction confirms the result. ∎

Thus, as long as the space infrastructure network is initiated by a core of sufficiently powerful actors (e.g., a coalition of the US, EU, and allies) and that initial stock exceeds the critical threshold, all rational actors will join, and the [Fund CDF] profile is the unique Nash equilibrium.

