APPENDIX C: DERIVATION OF THE OOD DEGRADATION FUNCTION AND EMPIRICAL ESTIMATION

C.1 KL‑Divergence Derivation

Let \mathcal{D}_{\text{earth}} be the probability density over environmental states encountered by AI during training, and \mathcal{D}_{\text{space}} the density over states encountered on the lunar or Martian frontier. We define the AI’s competence degradation as the exponential of the negative KL divergence:

\Gamma(\mathcal{D}_{\text{space}}) = \exp\left( -\beta_{\text{OOD}} \,\mathbb{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{space}} \| \mathcal{D}_{\text{earth}}) \right),

where \mathbb{D}_{KL}(P \| Q) = \int_\mathcal{X} p(x) \log\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\,dx measures the information‑theoretic surprise of space states relative to the training distribution. When the support of \mathcal{D}_{\text{space}} contains regions with q(x) = 0 but p(x) > 0, the KL divergence diverges, driving \Gamma \to 0. This corresponds precisely to Knightian uncertainty: novel phenomena for which no training data exists.

C.2 Empirical Bounds on KL Divergence for Lunar and Martian Environments

We propose a proxy using the expected number of novel sensor readings per mission hour compared to terrestrial robot operations. Terrestrial mobile robots in uncontrolled environments encounter OOD states with a frequency of approximately 0.01–0.1 per hour (states that significantly deviate from training distribution). For lunar operations, the frequency is estimated to be at least two orders of magnitude higher due to: (a) extreme temperature swings, (b) pervasive fine‑regolith electrostatics, (c) vacuum welding of mechanical joints, and (d) radiation‑induced bit‑flips creating unanticipated sensor data. Mars adds atmospheric dust dynamics, perchlorate chemistry, and biological potential signals that have no Earth analogue.

A conservative lower bound for lunar surface operations is:

\mathbb{D}_{KL}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{space}} \| \mathcal{D}_{\text{earth}}) \gtrsim 10^2 \text{ nats},

and for Mars it is higher still. Even with a moderate sensitivity \beta_{\text{OOD}} = 0.05, the degradation factor is \Gamma \lesssim e^{-5} \approx 0.007, reducing effective AI competence to below 1% of terrestrial levels. Combined with latency penalties, this makes autonomous AI cost‑ineffective for most physical tasks, as argued in Chapter 4.

