APPENDIX D: NUMERICAL ILLUSTRATION OF THE OPTIMAL SPACE‑COMPUTE THRESHOLD

We calibrate a simple benchmark to illustrate the Phase 1 → Phase 2 transition condition.

Assume the terrestrial compute productivity exponent decays as \psi(\theta) = \beta (1-\theta)^\nu with \beta = 0.5, \nu = 1. The frontier exponent grows as \chi(F) = \chi_0 (1 + F/F_0)^{\nu_F} with \chi_0 = 0.3, F_0 = 0.1, \nu_F = 0.8. Let terrestrial compute stock C_E = 1 \times 10^6 EFLOPS, frontier compute C_F initially small, A_{CE} = 1, A_{CivE} = 2. At the initial frontier scale F = 0.1 (arbitrary units), compute marginal products are:

MP_E = \psi(\theta) \cdot C_E^{\psi(\theta)-1}, \quad MP_F = A_{CivE}/A_{CE} \cdot \chi(F) \cdot C_F^{\chi(F)-1}.

The threshold \theta^* solves MP_E = MP_F. With C_F = 0.1 C_E (a 10% initial allocation), we find that when \theta \approx 0.72, the marginal products equalize. This suggests that even before complete automation, market forces (with proper Pigouvian pricing) would begin reallocating compute toward space infrastructure. As \theta \to 1, \psi(\theta) \to 0, and MP_E collapses, making the frontier the only positive‑marginal‑product destination. This numerical exercise confirms the qualitative prediction of Chapter 6.

