APPENDIX G: ADDITIONAL PROOFS AND LEMMAS

G.1 Unboundedness of Frontier Task Space

Lemma G.1 (Combinatorial Expansion). Let \mathcal{T}(F) be the number of open tasks on a frontier of scale F. If each solved task generates, on average, \alpha > 1 new tasks that were not previously identified, then \mathcal{T}(F) grows super‑exponentially with F.

Proof. The differential equation for tasks is \dot{\mathcal{T}} = (\alpha - 1) \dot{F}_s, where F_s is the number of solved tasks. Since each solved task opens new categories, the total task count grows faster than any exponential. ∎

G.2 Formal Proof of the Reinstatement Gap Divergence

Proposition G.2. For \theta(t) \to 1, the reinstatement gap G(t) = d\theta/dt - d\Theta_{\text{new}}/dt diverges to +\infty in the closed terrestrial economy because any newly created task is almost instantly automated (\dot\Theta_{\text{new}} \approx \dot\theta at a lower scale), while the displacement threshold continues to advance.

Proof. In the closed system, the set of automatable tasks expands monotonically; the reinstatement rate is bounded by the rate of creation of genuinely novel tasks, which cannot exceed the rate of human cognitive and institutional innovation. As AI approaches full generality, the lag between task creation and its automation tends to zero, so \dot\Theta_{\text{new}} < \dot\theta asymptotically. Hence G(t) > 0 for all large t. ∎

